nodecode-skill-retrieval is a rewrite of moonlight-lupin's moonlight-lupin/agent-skills:

  https://github.com/moonlight-lupin/agent-skills
  commit 79801c69e797f5f8efd619f8f451ca89d18c7240
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2025-2026 moonlight-lupin (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's skill retrieval, in Lisp against Nodecode's cell contract.
The corpus is a library of SKILL.md files under the section's roots - name and
description from frontmatter, triggers and tags indexed beside them, the folder
below its root as the id - ranked by the plugin's own BM25 (Okapi term
saturation, k1 1.5, b 0.75, and Lucene's always-positive IDF), rebuilt only when
a file's mtime or size moves. What the plugin did with a pre-LLM-call hook, this
cell does with the round hook: the turn's own input is ranked and the best k
skills are added to that very request, once per turn, under the same
`- **name** (id): description` lines. The same ranking is a tool,
`skill_search`, and the `/skill-search` command, so the model can ask on
demand.

Left out, and why: the plugin's prompt compaction and its optional Jev
reranking. Compaction rewrote Hermes' own `<available_skills>` block, which
Nodecode builds from its kept definitions - a different corpus this cell does
not own - and its per-session capability snapshots with it. The Jev stage POSTed
the query and short descriptions to a third-party service on every turn, which a
cell that promises to keep everything local should not do by default; BM25 alone
ranks here. The plugin's YAML reader (pyyaml) and its Hermes profile discovery
are gone too: Python is not bundled, the frontmatter members this cell indexes
are read by a few lines of Lisp, and the roots are the section's own.
